    depends on BT
    help
      This enables the DK Board specific initialization.
config AKIDATAG_BOARD
    bool "Enable AkidaTag board specific initialization"
    help
      This enables the AkidaTag board specific initialization.

config SYS_CPU_128MHZ
    bool "Run the nRF5340 application core at 128 MHz"
    default y
    help
      Raise the application-core HCLK divider to DIV1 so the CPU runs at its
      maximum 128 MHz instead of the 64 MHz reset default. Increases core
      dynamic power by roughly 2x. Only the CPU domain is affected; peripheral
      and system-timer clocks are unchanged. REQUIRED for SPIM4 at 32 MHz
      (the Zephyr driver clamps SPIM4 to 16 MHz unless HFCLK is DIV1) — so this
      is now default y for the DK too, not just the AkidaTag board.

config CURRENT_DEFAULT_RATE_HZ
    int "Default INA190 current-sense sampler rate (Hz)"
    default 10
    depends on AKIDATAG_BOARD
    help
      Default rate of the background current/power/energy sampler. Kept low for
      a stable reading and low overhead; accurate spot values come from
      `power measure`. Runtime-adjustable via the `power rate` shell command.

choice
    prompt "INA190 current-sense variant"
    default INA190_VARIANT_A3 if BOARD_REVISION = "2"
    default INA190_VARIANT_A1
    depends on AKIDATAG_BOARD
    help
      Fitted INA190 amplifier variant. The A1/A3 difference is gain only
      (A1 = 25 V/V, A3 = 100 V/V); the shunt resistors are board parts. The
      variant cannot be auto-detected (analog part, no board ID), so the default
      follows the board revision the build selects: A1 on revision 1, A3 on
      revision 2. Runtime-overridable via `power variant`.

config INA190_VARIANT_A1
    bool "INA190A1RSW (gain 25 V/V)"

config INA190_VARIANT_A3
    bool "INA190A3RSW (gain 100 V/V)"

endchoice

config AKD_SPI_FREQ_HZ
    int "AKD1500 host SPI clock (Hz)"
    default 8000000
    help
      Boot-time nRF5340 -> AKD1500 host SPI clock (both boards), applied after the
      AKD1500 SPI_S core is routed onto the PLL (akd_core_clock_to_pll) and then
      scaled to 5x this clock (akd_spi_set_clock) to keep a 25% ¼-rule margin.
      HW-validated at 8/16/32 MHz on SPIM4 (H0H1 drive). The nRF SPIM4
      rounds down to discrete steps (…/8/16/32 MHz; 32 MHz needs the 128 MHz core,
      capped at 32 MHz). Runtime-tunable via `spi_freq`; an app can override this
      default in its own .conf.

config AKD_CORE_CLOCK_HZ
    int "AKD1500 core clock (Hz)"
    default 400000000
    help
      Boot-time AKD1500 internal core clock (both boards), applied once after the
      SPI_S core is routed onto the PLL. 400 MHz (the default) is the reset value
      so it is a no-op. Values that divide 800 MHz evenly change only the SYS_DIV
      divider (SPI untouched); other values reprogram the PLL. Lower clocks trade
      compute speed for power. Runtime-tunable via `akd_coreclk`.

config AKD_WAKE_SETTLE_US
    int "AKD1500 settle delay after de-asserting SLEEP (us)"
    default 100
    help
      Busy-wait applied after waking the AKD1500 (the 0->1 transition of the
      akd_wake_get() reference count, which de-asserts SLEEP) and before the
      first SPI transaction. Only paid when the chip was actually asleep.

      AN-002 (Sleep and Low-Power Operation) prescribes a settle here without
      giving a figure: it says "If the first access after wake is unreliable,
      add a short settle delay after de-asserting SLEEP before the first
      transaction". No numeric spec is available to us, so this default is
      reasoned rather than quoted. SLEEP is a pure clock gate and AN-002 states
      the PLL and dividers stay active through sleep ("Fast wake"), so recovery
      is a gate release rather than a PLL relock: the settle only has to cover a
      few cycles of the slowest clock in the path. 100 us is over 2000 cycles of
      the 25 MHz reference, and is negligible beside the flash operations it
      precedes (a 14-sector erase measures ~286 ms). Raise it if a first access
      after wake is ever seen to be unreliable.

